WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.835
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.503
you know from her work on
"Westworld" and "Mildred Pierce"

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:05.505
and "The Wrestler."

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.973
She stars as Madonna
in the movie

00:00:07.973 --> 00:00:10.009
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story,"

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.812
which premieres November 4th
on Roku Channel.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.279
Let's take a look.

00:00:14.279 --> 00:00:16.116
-Okay, here's my thought.

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.383
I think this is
a really great idea.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.484
I hope you like it.

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:20.086
We team up, right?

00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:22.956
I'll write all the, you know,
good songs, the real ones,

00:00:22.956 --> 00:00:24.991
and then you parody them
every time.

00:00:24.991 --> 00:00:26.225
Bam! Bam!

00:00:26.225 --> 00:00:29.095
It's perfect.
[ Laughs ]

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.729
-Sounds great.
-It's fool-proof.

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:32.298
We'll be unstoppable.

00:00:32.298 --> 00:00:33.399
We'll be the
hottest power couple

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:35.402
in the entire music industry.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.370
What would our
power-couple name be?

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:40.072
I'm thinking Madyankovic

00:00:40.072 --> 00:00:41.607
or Madonnavic.

00:00:41.607 --> 00:00:45.010
Or maybe just Madonna.

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:48.982
-Please welcome back to the show
Evan Rachel Wood, everyone.

00:00:48.982 --> 00:00:50.683
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:02.028
Welcome back.
I'm so happy to have you.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:03.762
-I'm so happy to be back.
It's been a minute.

00:01:03.762 --> 00:01:05.298
-It has been a while.

00:01:05.298 --> 00:01:07.233
This is such a fun reason
to have you here.

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:11.570
This movie is a biopic
about Weird Al

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.639
that takes a lot of liberties
with the truth.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.007
-Oh, it's 100% accurate.

00:01:15.007 --> 00:01:16.576
I don't know
what you're talking about.

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:19.378
-Was it as much fun to make
as it was to watch?

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:22.582
-We had such a blast.
We had 18 days to shoot this.

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.984
-You shot it in 18 days?
That is no days.

00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:28.888
-It's zero days, yes.
It's like student-film days.

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.590
-Yes.
-But we did.

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:32.025
We had an amazing cast and crew.

00:01:32.025 --> 00:01:34.894
Daniel Radcliffe is, like,
an absolute dream to work with.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:37.763
And I'm addicted to
comedies now.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.666
I'm worried that I'm only
gonna want to do comedies now.

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:40.966
-Well, it was such a nice shift.

00:01:40.966 --> 00:01:44.303
I mean, you're obviously
a wonderful dramatic actress,

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:45.938
but it was so much fun to see

00:01:45.938 --> 00:01:46.939
you doing something
that seemed like --

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:48.775
-Is this what you guys do?

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.576
You show up
you and make each other laugh?

00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:52.077
You don't have to get
dragged through the mud

00:01:52.077 --> 00:01:53.445
or scream or anything?

00:01:53.445 --> 00:01:55.614
-Yeah, it was very -- You do get
dragged through the mud

00:01:55.614 --> 00:01:56.682
a great deal
in some of your work.

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:58.984
-Well, that is true. Yes, yes.

00:01:58.984 --> 00:02:03.823
-But you mentioned working
with Daniel, which is so cool.

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:05.591
And I know you have
a 9-year-old.

00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:08.828
Is your 9-year-old into
"Harry Potter"?

00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.463
Was that a big deal that
you were working with Daniel?

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:13.465
-Of course, yes. Definitely.

00:02:13.465 --> 00:02:16.101
We watched all of the
"Harry Potter" films together.

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:19.439
And I told him I would be
working with Daniel Radcliffe.

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:22.342
And he said,
"Oh, can I come to set?"

00:02:22.342 --> 00:02:28.314
And I went, "Ah, no," because
I didn't know how to tell my son

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:32.718
that part of my job was
to just violently make out

00:02:32.718 --> 00:02:35.822
with him
throughout the entire film.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:38.224
I'm like,
"You can't come and watch mom

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.660
make out with Harry Potter, no."

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.462
-You're like,
"Even wizards grow up."

00:02:43.462 --> 00:02:46.065
-Yeah. And -- Exactly.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:49.435
And, you know,
but no playing with wands.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:51.070
We're not going to do this, no.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.739
-So, you play Madonna
in the film.

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:53.806
-Yes.

00:02:53.806 --> 00:02:55.340
-And this is a -- You know,

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:58.778
there have been so many eras in
her iconic music career,

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.679
but this seemed like
a particularly fun era.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:01.947
One of your favorite
Madonna eras?

00:03:01.947 --> 00:03:03.416
-Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:03:03.416 --> 00:03:05.751
I mean, I've been such a huge
fan of hers since I was a kid.

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.087
I would, you know,
fake sick so I could

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.889
stay home from school
to lip-sync

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:11.223
"The Immaculate Collection."

00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:13.426
And I totally got busted by
my mom once, like,

00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:17.196
in a mid-knee slide with
the candlestick microphone.

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:18.464
And, you know, I was like...

00:03:18.464 --> 00:03:20.400
♪ Like a Pra-- ♪

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.068
And she was like,
"You're not sick, go to school."

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:25.271
So, yeah, I've been training
my whole life for this.

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.406
-How do you prep to
play Madonna?

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:30.109
Especially because there's got
to be a level of accuracy,

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:32.511
but, also, it's a comedy,
so you don't have to take it

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:33.779
as seriously as a biopic.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.948
But you also
can't just mail it in.

00:03:35.948 --> 00:03:39.051
-No. And I did take it
very seriously.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.087
It felt like the pressure
was slightly off

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:43.690
because it wasn't
a Madonna biopic.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:44.824
It was a parody.

00:03:44.824 --> 00:03:46.359
But, yes,
I still wanted it to be good.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:47.960
I kind of wanted,
you know, people to be like,

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.296
"Yeah, you know, the Madonna
biopic was really good,

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:52.131
but did you see
Evan Rachel Wood do

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.800
the Madonna in
the Weird Al movie?"

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.101
Like, that was kind of the goal.

00:03:56.101 --> 00:04:00.506
-You have been lucky enough
to meet the real Madonna?

00:04:00.506 --> 00:04:03.141
-Yes. Yes.
-And wow was your time with her?

00:04:03.141 --> 00:04:06.011
-We had a lovely time together.
We had dinner once.

00:04:06.011 --> 00:04:07.380
We've been very friendly.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.248
I don't know if she'll be
calling me after this,

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:12.451
because I didn't know that they
didn't have her permission.

00:04:12.451 --> 00:04:13.986
Once I signed on,

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:15.454
I found out we were like
halfway through shooting.

00:04:15.454 --> 00:04:16.856
-Yeah. On day nine?

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:19.058
-Day nine, I was like,
"So, what did Madonna say

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:20.359
when you asked her about this?"

00:04:20.359 --> 00:04:22.061
And they were like,
"We never talked to Madonna."

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:25.131
I was like, "Great."

00:04:25.131 --> 00:04:28.368
-It will be very funny
if you get a text from her

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:31.070
that makes it very clear
that she's seen the film.

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.105
-Or I don't get
a Christmas card this year.

00:04:33.105 --> 00:04:34.874
-Yes, exactly.

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.743
But it is such a --
And, again, that era, I think,

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:39.645
for everybody --
I mean, Daniel obviously has

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:41.947
a very fun wardrobe, as well,
getting to be Weird Al.

00:04:41.947 --> 00:04:45.017
But dressing up like Madonna
from the '80s --

00:04:45.017 --> 00:04:46.085
it's so much fun.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:47.453
-I never wanted to take
the clothes off.

00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.055
And every day,
I came to work thinking like,

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.189
"I wonder if this is
ever going to get old

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.457
or I'm gonna get jaded
about this."

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.292
Nope. It was always just as fun.

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.094
I couldn't stop doing
Dubsmashes.

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.496
Couldn't stop listening to
Madonna music.

00:04:57.496 --> 00:04:59.164
We all had a great time,
especially, like,

00:04:59.164 --> 00:05:00.500
hair and makeup, as well.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.903
-You mentioned your kid
getting to meet Daniel.

00:05:03.903 --> 00:05:06.072
Have you worked with
other co-stars over the years

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.706
that are impressive to
a 9-year-old?

00:05:07.706 --> 00:05:09.142
-Yes, absolutely.

00:05:09.142 --> 00:05:13.879
I mean, he could barely get out
a word when he met Daniel.

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:16.215
-And it should be noted,
Daniel, I would imagine,

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:19.518
has been through that before
and is probably the best at it,

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:20.920
maybe better than
anyone in the world.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:22.421
He's the sweetest guy
in the world.

00:05:22.421 --> 00:05:24.323
And he's very aware that
when he meets kids,

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:26.224
it's going to be harder for
the kid than it is for him.

00:05:26.224 --> 00:05:29.429
-Exactly. I mean, I can't even
imagine how many times

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:30.829
he's been asked to do that.

00:05:30.829 --> 00:05:32.164
Handled it so well.

00:05:32.164 --> 00:05:34.700
I think my child
got out maybe three words.

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.568
And I was like,
"Daniel, thank you so much.

00:05:36.568 --> 00:05:38.471
You know, sorry, you know,
they only said three things."

00:05:38.471 --> 00:05:40.372
And he went,
"Oh, that's impressive.

00:05:40.372 --> 00:05:43.509
Three words is very impressive.
Most kids just hide.

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.810
I don't even see them."

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.712
And so I was like,
"Okay, you did good."

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:48.915
But I think the only other time
he really geeked out

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:51.984
was when after
I had done "Frozen 2"

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:54.754
and he got to meet Josh Gad,

00:05:54.754 --> 00:05:57.022
who does the voice of Olaf,
and didn't believe that he was

00:05:57.022 --> 00:05:59.725
Olaf until he was like,
"Alright, close your eyes."

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:02.427
And then he whispered,
"I'm Olaf and I like warm hugs."

00:06:02.427 --> 00:06:06.298
And my child just went...

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:08.167
And, like, you know,
major mom points.

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:10.369
-It can break different ways,
because every now and then,

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:12.137
I've been lucky enough to --
A friend of mine has done

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:14.039
a voiceover, and I do the same
thing with my kids.

00:06:14.039 --> 00:06:16.708
And I think, sometimes,
they don't love it.

00:06:16.708 --> 00:06:18.043
Like, they like to picture

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:19.478
the sort of clean lines
of animation,

00:06:19.478 --> 00:06:22.648
and when they meet, like,
my scraggly dope friends,

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:24.549
they're like,
"Oh, I don't like this at all.

00:06:24.549 --> 00:06:26.485
-"Ooh! That's not --" No.

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:28.587
-"I don't like that you
were in my iPad."

00:06:28.587 --> 00:06:30.623
-I can imagine.

00:06:30.623 --> 00:06:32.491
-You have a band.
-Yes.

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:35.328
-And your band
performed on Halloween.

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:37.330
-We did. We performed in
New York on Halloween.

00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:38.798
-Have you often done
Halloween shows?

00:06:38.798 --> 00:06:40.099
-We do a Halloween show
every year.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.167
-Fantastic.
-Yes.

00:06:41.167 --> 00:06:42.768
-I'm so jealous
at the fact you have

00:06:42.768 --> 00:06:44.470
Halloween plans that you know
what they're gonna be.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.972
-Every year.
-Yeah.

00:06:46.972 --> 00:06:49.142
And you get to be onstage,
and I would imagine

00:06:49.142 --> 00:06:50.242
people are having
the best possible time

00:06:50.242 --> 00:06:51.276
at a show like that.

00:06:51.276 --> 00:06:52.611
-They are.

00:06:52.611 --> 00:06:53.746
And this was the first time we
had done a Halloween show

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:54.947
in New York.

00:06:54.947 --> 00:06:57.416
And I always get, like,
slightly intimidated

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:59.418
because I love New York
and I always want to impress

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:01.887
the crowd, but I always forget
what a great audience they are.

00:07:01.887 --> 00:07:03.221
They're so receptive.

00:07:03.221 --> 00:07:05.424
They're, like, theater people.
They love a good show.

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:06.859
And so it was great.
We had an amazing time.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:07.960
Everyone was singing along.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.228
-Are you costumed on a
Halloween show?

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:10.296
-Oh, yes.
-Okay.

00:07:10.296 --> 00:07:13.366
This year, we dressed as
Miriam and John

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:15.101
from the movie "The Hunger."

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.536
It's a vampire movie,
yeah, from the '80s.

00:07:16.536 --> 00:07:17.603
-[ Laughs ]
-What are you laughing?

00:07:17.603 --> 00:07:20.839
-That is the deepest cut.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:22.875
Oh, my God, Miriam and John
from "The Hunger."

00:07:22.875 --> 00:07:26.179
I saw so many of those
this year.

00:07:26.179 --> 00:07:28.213
-We're a niche band, you know?
-Yeah, you're a niche band.

00:07:28.213 --> 00:07:29.482
Right, right.

00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:31.517
And then, when you look out
in the audience, though,

00:07:31.517 --> 00:07:34.286
do you feel like your audience
are niche with their costumes

00:07:34.286 --> 00:07:35.954
or are you like,
"Oh, there's Mario.

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:37.056
There's Waldo."

00:07:37.056 --> 00:07:39.324
-Well, I did see
a Judy Funnie from "Doug."

00:07:39.324 --> 00:07:41.093
-Okay.
-That was a deep cut for me.

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:44.530
But I also see a lot of Dolori
is what I like to call them,

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:46.765
dressing up like
Dolores from "Westworld."

00:07:46.765 --> 00:07:48.767
-Oh, that must be weird.

00:07:48.767 --> 00:07:50.503
-A lot of blue dresses.
Yeah.

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:51.737
A lot of Dolori in the audience.

00:07:51.737 --> 00:07:53.872
-Now, when you're performing for
people in costume,

00:07:53.872 --> 00:07:56.108
do you, as a performer,
know there are people in costume

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:58.544
or do you allow yourself to
believe that those characters,

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.546
those fictional characters,
have come to see you in a show?

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:02.214
-Wow. You know,
I never thought about that,

00:08:02.214 --> 00:08:03.549
but the next time I do a show,

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:07.286
I'm absolutely
going to think that.

00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:08.787
-Well, look,
congratulations on the movie.

00:08:08.787 --> 00:08:10.789
It is really so much fun and,

00:08:10.789 --> 00:08:13.025
like I was saying backstage,
such a funny idea.

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:14.827
The execution
is so much more impressive.

00:08:14.827 --> 00:08:16.562
I was delighted by how many
twists and turns there were.

00:08:16.562 --> 00:08:17.629
And you're really great.

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:18.964
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:08:18.964 --> 00:08:20.166
-Thanks for having me.
-Always a pleasure to see you.

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:22.702
-Yeah.
-Evan Rachel Wood, everybody.

00:08:22.702 --> 00:08:25.838
"Weird: The Al Yankovic Story"
premieres tomorrow

00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:27.039
on The Roku Channel.

00:08:27.039 --> 00:08:28.541
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

